ey're trying to create fire barriers  they're plowing up digging up huge edges  of the forest to stop the spread because  you have some roads which are completely  burnt out and then it's pretty pristine  on the other side and the worry is if  fire spreads to the pristine areas where  they can't access access it as well it  will become a massive problem and it  will it would take weeks and weeks to  control so at the moment as i say  the winds are starting to build and  that's the worry you've got these hot  strong winds building up this heat wave  40 degree or so heat at the moment all  of that making these fires difficult  exhausting and particularly  unpredictable and volatile  and just very briefly you kind of  answered it there but any end in sight  in terms of the weather in the next week  or so just briefly  i spoke to the chief of the fire and she  said to me we just live in hope there is  rain potentially forecast in the next  few days but she said i'll be honest  with you we need two weeks of english  rain if we were to properly bring these  fires under control  well thank you very much indeed it's a  very very powerful stuff well as we  mentioned earlier this is a europe-wide  drought meaning the continent's big  rivers are dwindling islands are  appearing in the loire and in germany  drought hits business too it is a vital  trade route there and jane secker is on  the banks hi tom well i'm at the rhine  germany's commercial artery this is the  river that transports goods up and down  the country and it carries everything  food fuel coal metal stuff for heavy  industry  cars we've seen them all coming past us  today on the river but where i'm  standing is not the river bank it is the  river bed uh normally there'll be water  here there'll be little fish swimming  around here but it is becoming like a  beach and as this is because there's  been no rain and the river levels have  fallen but as the river shrinks in terms  of width it also shrinks in terms of