ons of pounds of new  spending has been announced  in the last couple of years about how to  tackle that problem we've all come to  accept that our winters are extremely  hotter and wetter i think there's just  been less of a conversation about  everything getting hotter and drier  well i know one group that particularly  has been pleading for that conversation  for a long time people can concerned  with rivers the rivers trust and the  anglers as well have have been pointing  out that a lot of our rivers have kind  of turned into lengthy puddles that  there's very little flow there the  levels are artificially maintained some  of them i think it's uh three are incred  sorry six around the country from the  rather in yorkshire down to the waveney  here in in uh in suffolk or away to the  east here and others as well are  incredibly low and they say the  environment's been paying the price for  the fact that we've just been kind of  sucking so much water out of the ground  and really suggesting that can't  continue there are some tricky questions  here for the for the public as well  aren't there about our own behavior  yes as well as the importance i think  that's absolutely right of managing you  know for example wetlands better which  we're told by everyone who's white on  these matters are both the solution to  to helping communities deal with  flooding and the solution to helping  communities deal with drought  but there's also this difficult  conversation to not just to do with  supply and investment in new pipe works  and so on and but to do with our our  consumption of water just as there is  now a big conversation about consumption  of energy uh you know why doesn't  everybody in the uk for example collect  rainwater as a matter of course why  hasn't there been a big public messaging  campaign around all of that and the  answer i think is that as a number of  people who work in this policy space  have said to me you know britain still  thinks of itself as a 